# 8. AI-POWERED MATCHING ENGINE

This is a central feature. Implement it as a modular, testable service.

The system should compare lost-item reports against found-item reports.

## Text analysis

Analyze:
- Item title.
- Category.
- Description.
- Brand.
- Color.
- Distinguishing features.
- Date.
- Location.
- Other available non-sensitive metadata.

Normalize text and handle minor spelling differences, synonyms, and variations in descriptions.

## Image analysis

When both reports contain photographs, analyze image similarity using a suitable configured vision model or image embeddings.

The model should compare relevant visual features such as:
- Overall appearance.
- Shape.
- Color.
- Visible brand or design.
- Distinctive accessories or markings.

Image similarity is only supporting evidence. Two objects looking similar does not prove that they are the same object.

Do not claim computer vision is operational if it is only represented by mock data.

## Combined matching score

Design a configurable scoring system that combines:
- Text similarity.
- Category compatibility.
- Visual similarity, when available.
- Color and brand agreement.
- Location relevance.
- Date compatibility.
- Distinguishing-feature agreement.

Normalize the final score to a 0–100 scale.

Use configurable thresholds:
- High-confidence candidate.
- Possible candidate.
- Low-confidence candidate.

Determine sensible default thresholds during implementation and document them. Do not represent these scores as statistically calibrated probabilities unless they have actually been calibrated.

Missing information should not automatically count as a contradiction.

A missing photograph should never prevent a valid text-based match.

## Matching workflow

When a new lost or found report is submitted:
1. Retrieve eligible, active reports of the opposite type.
2. Apply basic category and status filters.
3. Compare available text and images.
4. Rank candidates by matching score.
5. Save match records and supporting evidence.
6. Avoid duplicate active match records.
7. Notify relevant users about sufficiently strong matches.
8. Allow users to dismiss an incorrect suggestion.
9. Re-run matching when relevant report details change.

Include a matching service interface so the implementation can start with a deterministic text-based baseline and later use a real vision or embedding provider.

If AI credentials are unavailable, provide a clearly labeled demo mode or functional fallback. Never silently fabricate AI results.

