# 9. COMPLETE ITEM-RETURN AND VERIFICATION WORKFLOW

Implement the following workflow as an explicit state machine. Every transition must be validated by the backend.

## Stage 1: Potential match discovered

The AI identifies a possible match between a lost-item report and a found-item report.

The lost-item owner receives an in-app notification:

"Possible match found! A found item may match your lost [item name]. Review the details to see whether it could be yours."

The notification should include:
- Relevant report information.
- Match score or confidence category.
- Available image preview.
- Supporting similarities.
- A button to review the match.

Do not disclose private information from the other user unnecessarily.

## Stage 2: Lost-item owner reviews the suggestion

Show the candidate match in a dedicated review page.

The owner can:
- "This might be mine".
- "Not my item".

If the owner rejects it:
- Record the decision.
- Mark that candidate as dismissed for the relevant user and reports.
- Avoid repeatedly sending the same rejected suggestion unless the underlying evidence materially changes.

If the owner accepts the suggestion:
- Create or activate a claim request.
- Notify the finder that someone has expressed interest in claiming the item.
- Move the request into the verification stage.

The acceptance of a potential match is not final proof of ownership.

## Stage 3: Finder asks a verification question

The finder opens the claim request and asks a custom question about the item.

Examples of suitable questions:
- "What is the sticker on the back of the laptop?"
- "What is written on the keychain?"
- "Can you describe the mark near the zipper?"

Do not automatically reveal secret distinguishing details from the original found report.

The finder should choose a question that helps distinguish the actual owner from someone making an unsupported claim.

Save the question and its associated request.

## Stage 4: Lost-item owner receives the question

Send an in-app notification:

"The finder needs one more detail to verify your claim."

The notification links to the claim verification page.

The lost-item owner can read the question and submit an answer.

Allow the owner to submit a text answer and, when useful, supporting evidence.

The owner should not see the finder's private account information unnecessarily.

## Stage 5: Finder reviews the answer

Notify the finder when the answer arrives.

The finder reviews the submitted answer and chooses:
- Accept answer.
- Request clarification.
- Reject answer.

If accepted:
- Mark the verification as successful.
- Allow the finder to proceed to arranging the return.

If clarification is requested:
- Notify the owner.
- Allow another answer.
- Preserve the previous response in the audit history.

If rejected:
- Record the decision and reason where appropriate.
- Keep the claim in a reviewable state.
- Provide a safe dispute or campus-moderator escalation option.

Apply reasonable limits to repeated questions and attempts. Do not treat a successful AI similarity score as proof of ownership.

## Stage 6: Finder proposes a handover

After successful verification, the finder can propose:
- A campus meeting location.
- A date.
- A time.
- Optional instructions.

Suggested safe meeting locations:
- Campus security office.
- Library reception.
- Main administration office.
- An approved public campus location.

Only display institution-specific locations when configured by the administrator. Otherwise, allow users to enter a safe public campus location.

The finder submits the handover proposal.

Notify the lost-item owner:

"Your claim has been verified! The finder has proposed a place and time to return your item."

The owner can:
- Confirm the proposal.
- Request a different time or location.

Notify the other participant when the proposal is confirmed or changed.

Do not mark the item as returned just because a meeting has been scheduled.

## Stage 7: Physical handover

Display a clear handover status to both parties.

Possible statuses:
- Awaiting verification.
- Verification successful.
- Handover proposed.
- Handover confirmed.
- Awaiting handover confirmation.
- Returned.
- Closed.

After the physical exchange, allow both participants to confirm that the handover took place.

The lost-item owner should have a prominent "I Received My Item" button.

The finder should also have a "Handover Completed" action.

Use these confirmations to reduce accidental or false closures. If the participants disagree, allow them to request moderator assistance.

## Stage 8: Close the query

Once the required handover confirmation conditions are satisfied, the lost-item owner can close the query.

On closure:
- Mark the claim as completed.
- Mark the lost report as recovered.
- Update the found report's availability and status.
- Close associated active matching requests as appropriate.
- Preserve the history of the completed case.
- Remove it from active matching.
- Notify both participants.
- Show a success screen.

Suggested message:

"Item successfully returned! Thank you for helping reconnect our campus community."

Provide an option to return to the dashboard.

## Workflow integrity

Implement backend validation for every transition. Prevent unauthorized users from accepting claims, answering another user's question, confirming another user's actions, or changing reports they do not own.

Handle expired, cancelled, rejected, disputed, and already-closed requests without breaking the workflow.

Show a visual progress tracker on every claim detail page so both participants understand the current stage and next action.

