- The first impression is a beautiful campus-themed FindItAi landing page inspired by the supplied reference.
- Signup and login are functional.
- Students and faculty can report lost and found items.
- The system identifies and displays plausible matches using a clearly implemented matching service.
- The owner can accept or reject a suggested match.
- The finder can ask a verification question.
- The owner can answer it.
- The finder can review the answer.
- Both participants can coordinate a handover.
- The handover and query can be properly completed.
- Notifications are linked to real application events.
- Permissions and workflow transitions are enforced by the backend.
- The application is responsive and installable as a PWA.
- The complete workflow can be demonstrated using clearly labeled demo data.
- The source code is organized and maintainable.

Begin by creating the application foundation and implementing the landing page, authentication screens, and design system. Then continue with the functional backend and complete lost-and-found workflow. Keep the visual quality high throughout the entire application.

Do not respond with only a plan, tutorial, wireframe, or code snippets. Build the application in the available environment and clearly explain any capabilities that require external setup.